{"content":[{"c":["ARIMATHEA"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Hometown of the Joseph who obtained Jesus’ crucified body and buried it in his own tomb (",{"href":"?bref=Matt.27.57","r":"Matt.27.57","rawR":"Matt.27.57","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.27.57","value":"Matt.27.57"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mt 27:57"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Mark.15.43","r":"Mark.15.43","rawR":"Mark.15.43","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mark.15.43","value":"Mark.15.43"}],"t":"ref","v":"Mk 15:43"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=John.19.38","r":"John.19.38","rawR":"John.19.38","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"John.19.38","value":"John.19.38"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jn 19:38"},"). The town’s location is unknown, though it may be identical to the hill town of Ramathaim-zophim, the prophet Samuel’s home (",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.1.1","r":"1Sam.1.1","rawR":"1Sam.1.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.1.1","value":"1Sam.1.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sm 1:1"},"), about 8 miles (12.9 kilometers) northwest of Jerusalem. Luke described the place as a Jewish town, and Joseph was himself a Jewish official (",{"href":"?bref=Luke.23.50","r":"Luke.23.50","rawR":"Luke.23.50","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Luke.23.50","value":"Luke.23.50"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lk 23:50"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Arimathea","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Mt 27:57","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Matt.27.57","rawQueryValue":"Matt.27.57","sourceRefExact":"Matt.27.57"},{"anchorText":"Mk 15:43","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Mark.15.43","rawQueryValue":"Mark.15.43","sourceRefExact":"Mark.15.43"},{"anchorText":"Jn 19:38","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=John.19.38","rawQueryValue":"John.19.38","sourceRefExact":"John.19.38"},{"anchorText":"1 Sm 1:1","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionar